<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 691</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr was a brother of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a> and his governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a>. Ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtar-al-thaqafi">al-Mukhtar al-Thaqafi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> in 687. He was killed in 691 fighting the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik at Maskin, which returned Iraq to Umayyad rule.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mus'ab was a son of Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, a prominent companion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>, and of Rabab bint Unayf, a daughter of a chieftain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kalb">Banu Kalb</a>. In the last years of the caliph Mu'awiya I he belonged to a group that met in the mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, among them his half-brother Urwa and the future Umayyad caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a>. His wives included A'isha, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talha-ibn-ubayd-allah">Talha ibn Ubayd Allah</a>, and Sukayna, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the death of the caliph Yazid in November 683, Mus'ab's half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a> was recognised as caliph in most of the Caliphate. Mus'ab led an expedition against Umayyad Palestine in 684/685, which Amr ibn Sa'id al-Ashdaq repulsed, and then governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtar-al-thaqafi">Mukhtar al-Thaqafi</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> in 685, Ibn al-Zubayr made Mus'ab governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in 686 to recover Kufa and fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a>, and Mus'ab sent his commander Muhallab ibn Abi Sufra against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azariqa">Azariqa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and Kirman.</p><p>After defeating Mukhtar's army at Madhar and Harura, Mus'ab besieged him in the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> for four months. Between 6,000 and 8,000 of Mukhtar's supporters surrendered, and under pressure from the vengeful tribal nobles Mus'ab executed them all, which left Kufa resentful. Briefly removed by his brother after complaints and then restored, he put down the rebellion of Ubayd Allah ibn al-Hurr al-Ju'fi, faced Abd al-Malik in campaigns of 689 and 690 that ended without battle, and harshly punished Umayyad loyalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> after a revolt there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 691 Abd al-Malik camped at Maskin. Mus'ab could raise only a small army, and Abd al-Malik won over most of his commanders with promises of money and governorships. Mus'ab ignored a warning from Ibrahim ibn al-Ashtar, and when Ibn al-Ashtar fell early in the battle the rest refused to fight or defected. His son Isa was killed before his eyes. Mus'ab refused an offer of amnesty and was killed by Za'ida ibn Qudama al-Thaqafi, a follower of Mukhtar. He was buried at Dayr al-Jathaliq, where a mausoleum over his grave became a pilgrimage site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He governed from Mecca, where he rebuilt the Kaaba after it was damaged in an Umayyad siege, while his brother <b>Mus'ab</b> ruled Iraq from Basra.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in Dayr al-Jathaliq. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=681" aria-label="Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5778 5697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5778 5697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.42,39.83&amp;year=681">What was here in 681 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
